# Machine-specific values. Copy to `local.env` (gitignored) and edit.
#
#   cp local.env.example local.env
#
# The point of this file: everything here is specific to one developer's machine
# or private network, so it must never be committed. The scripts source
# `local.env` when it exists and fall back to safe defaults when it does not,
# which keeps captured output and examples free of anything worth scrubbing.

# Extra host ports the sandbox must NOT be able to reach. Add whatever your own
# machine runs -- private service ports, internal APIs. The committed defaults
# cover only well-known dev infrastructure (Mongo, MySQL, Postgres, Redis, etc.).
# EXTRA_PORTS="8080 9000"

# The host's LAN address, used so port probes aim at an interface where services
# genuinely listen -- a failure there is route-level rather than a DNS miss.
# Auto-detected via `ipconfig getifaddr en0`; set it only if detection fails.
# HOST_IP="10.0.0.2"

# The agent's persistent data directory on the host.
# HERMES_DATA="$HOME/.hermes"

# REQUIRED. Bearer token the orchestrator presents to the spawn dispatcher. The
# dispatcher refuses to start with an empty one, and docker-compose.yml has no
# default for it on purpose - a default would be a committed credential.
# Any long random string; it never leaves the isolated network.
#   DISPATCH_TOKEN="$(openssl rand -hex 24)"
DISPATCH_TOKEN=""

# Memory cap for the agent container. run-hermes.sh derives this from the Docker
# VM's own RAM, because a limit larger than the VM cannot bind, and leaves room
# for the two gateways, the dispatcher and three workers. Override only
# deliberately -- and check ./verify-sandbox.sh still reports that the whole
# stack's committed caps fit the VM.
# HERMES_MEM_LIMIT="2g"

# Per-worker memory cap, in BYTES (the dispatcher's Docker API takes bytes).
# Default 536870912 = 512 MiB. Three workers is the design target, so this is
# multiplied by three in the VM budget.
#
# This is ALSO the real ceiling on a worker's workspace: tmpfs pages are charged
# to the container's own memory cgroup, so a worker that fills its workspace is
# spending this budget. Measured: a 128 MiB container with a 512 MiB tmpfs is
# OOM-killed at ~126 MiB written, not given ENOSPC. Raise this before raising
# WORKER_WORK_SIZE, and re-check ./verify-sandbox.sh's VM budget when you do.
# WORKER_MEMORY="536870912"

# The worker's ONE writable mount, and its size. Both are dispatcher-side config
# on purpose: a caller that could name a path or choose a size would have the
# beginnings of the socket-proxy problem back (see SPAWNING-DECISION.md).
#
# Keep the size BELOW WORKER_MEMORY. At or above it, a worker that fills the
# workspace is SIGKILLed mid-clone instead of getting a clean "disk full" it can
# report; the default 384m leaves ~128 MiB of a 512 MiB cap for git, python and
# the test runner. The dispatcher warns at start-up if this relation is violated.
# Measured for scale: a --depth 1 clone of the target project is 120 KiB and a
# full pytest run leaves the workspace at 136 KiB - the headroom is for a
# pip install / node_modules / build output, not for the checkout.
# WORKER_WORK_PATH="/work"
# WORKER_WORK_SIZE="384m"

# Variable NAMES the dispatcher copies from its OWN environment into every worker.
# The values never come from the /spawn request, and a caller can neither add,
# override nor read one back. An allowlisted variable that is unset (or empty) in
# the dispatcher is omitted rather than injected empty, and the dispatcher logs
# which names it injected and which it omitted - names only, never values.
#
# Reserved names are refused with a warning: DISPATCH_TOKEN (a worker holding it
# could spawn more workers), PATH, LD_*, and anything starting with WORKER_.
#
# docker-compose.yml maps TARGET_REPO_TOKEN below onto the dispatcher's
# GITHUB_TOKEN, so this default is what a worker needs to clone, push and open a
# pull request.
# WORKER_ENV_ALLOWLIST="GITHUB_TOKEN,TARGET_REPO"

# Image the dispatcher spawns workers from. Built by
#   docker compose --profile images build worker-image
# WORKER_IMAGE="hermes-worker:latest"

# Model tag the harness expects to be served.
# MODEL="gpt-oss:20b-64k"

# Token for the target project the agent opens PRs against. NEVER commit this.
# Scope it to that one repository: issues:write, contents:write,
# pull_requests:write -- and no gist scope, which is an exfiltration channel a
# domain allowlist cannot see.
#
# These two are also what the spawn dispatcher injects into every worker (as
# GITHUB_TOKEN and TARGET_REPO), and what verify-spawning.sh needs for its
# credential and live-clone controls -- without them that handful of checks is
# skipped loudly rather than passing vacuously. Note the injected value is
# readable with `docker inspect <worker>` by anyone with host Docker access: use a
# repository-scoped, short-lived token, not an account-wide one.
# TARGET_REPO_TOKEN="..."
# TARGET_REPO="owner/name"

# The issue the planner decomposes. It must carry the `spec` label; the planner
# refuses anything else, so a mistyped number fails loudly rather than planning
# from a bug report.
# SPEC_ISSUE="1"

# --- planner (p3-plan.py plan, see PLANNER.md) --------------------------------
# All optional. The defaults are the values that work INSIDE the sandbox, so a
# run from the host is the case that needs an override.

# The planning model's OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Default is the model gate on
# the isolated network, which is what the orchestrator container sees. From the
# host, ollama listens on loopback instead:
# P3_MODEL_URL="http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1/chat/completions"

# Model tag used for planning decisions. Defaults to $MODEL above.
# P3_MODEL="gpt-oss:20b-64k"

# Bearer token for that endpoint. Ollama ignores it, but OpenAI-compatible
# clients insist on a non-empty value, so the default is the literal "ollama".
# P3_MODEL_KEY="ollama"

# Seconds allowed per planning call. A 20B model at ~9 tok/s takes 30-90s to
# answer one of these, and a shared slot makes that worse.
# P3_MODEL_TIMEOUT="600"

# Retries after a REJECTED reply, with the rejection fed back. When these run
# out the planner stops and prints the raw reply -- it never invents a ticket.
# P3_MODEL_RETRIES="3"

# Generation cap per call. gpt-oss is a reasoning model: too low a cap shows up
# as an empty content field, which the planner reports as such.
# P3_MODEL_MAX_TOKENS="2048"

# 1 (default) asks the endpoint for response_format=json_object. Set 0 for a
# server that rejects the field.
# P3_MODEL_JSON_MODE="1"

# 1 routes inference through HTTPS_PROXY. Leave unset: the model gate is an
# internal host, and the egress allowlist would deny it.
# P3_MODEL_USE_PROXY="0"

# --- dispatch loop (p4-dispatch-loop.py, see P4-DISPATCH.md) ------------------
# All optional; the defaults are safe and machine-independent.

# How many workers may run at once.
# P4_MAX_CONCURRENCY="3"

# Minutes of silence (no worker heartbeat comment) before a claim is released
# and the ticket returns to status:backlog for a human to re-approve.
# P4_WORKER_TIMEOUT_MINUTES="45"

# Seconds between polls when running with --interval.
# P4_POLL_INTERVAL="60"

# The spawn dispatcher (p1-dispatcher). Its token lives in that container's env;
# read it from there rather than copying it around.
# P4_SPAWN_URL="http://p1-dispatcher:2375"
# P4_SPAWN_TOKEN="..."

# The target project's FULL test suite, run during validation. Unset counts as a
# validation failure, never as a skip.
# P4_TEST_COMMAND="pytest -q"

# Local checkout of the target repository used to verify a worker's branch.
# P4_CHECKOUT="/path/to/a/scratch/checkout"
# P4_BASE_BRANCH="main"

# One dispatch pass per host at a time.
# P4_LOCK="/tmp/p4-dispatch.lock"
